## The finding

`crime_00008` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path**, `src/api/files.ts:12–15`, detector `sync_io_in_hotpath.js`, confidence `0.9`.

Summary, verbatim:

> "3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured."

Evidence, verbatim:

> - "`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
> - "`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
> - "`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
> - "lines: 12, 13, 15"
> - "swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it"

## The blocking calls

Three, all from `node:fs`, all inside `loadPlanSchema()`:

| Line | Call | What it blocks on |
|---|---|---|
| 12 | `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | a `stat` syscall |
| 13 | `statSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | a second `stat` syscall on the same path |
| 15 | `readFileSync(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8")` | `open` + `read` + `close` of the whole file |

That's **three synchronous filesystem round-trips per invocation**, and Node's `*Sync` family runs on the main thread — not the libuv threadpool. Every concurrent request queued behind this one waits for all three. Under load the cost isn't the file read itself, it's the serialization: N concurrent requests become N sequential disk trips, which is exactly the p99-blowout-plus-event-loop-lag shape described. `JSON.parse` on line 15 is main-thread too, and it can't even start until the blocking read returns.

Note the file is currently uncalled in-repo (`grep` for `loadPlanSchema` finds only the declaration), so nothing constrains the migration beyond the exported contract.

## Smallest async migration

One honest caveat up front: **`(): unknown` cannot stay `(): unknown`.** Node has no way to do non-blocking I/O and return a synchronous value — the only "async" escapes that preserve a sync return (`Atomics.wait`, a sync worker round-trip) block the event loop just as hard, which is the thing you're trying to stop. So the minimal change keeps the export name and the empty parameter list identical, and widens the return to `Promise<unknown>`:

```ts
import { readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {
  let info;
  try {
    info = await stat(SCHEMA_PATH);
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
  if (info.size === 0) return null;
  return JSON.parse(await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8"));
}
```

What this preserves:

- **Same export, same arity, same three outcomes** — `null` for missing, `null` for empty, parsed value otherwise.
- **Same error behaviour on bad JSON.** `JSON.parse` stays outside the `catch`, so malformed schemas still throw rather than silently becoming `null`.
- **`existsSync` folds into `stat`.** `existsSync` was already just a swallowed `stat`; the `catch` reproduces it exactly, including the permission-error case where `existsSync` also returns `false`. Three syscalls become two, and both now run on the threadpool.

Call sites need one `await` added. Since there are none today, the migration is a single-file change.

**Optional follow-up, not part of the minimal fix:** collapse to one syscall by reading straight into a `try` and treating any read failure as `null` — but keep `JSON.parse` outside the `try`, or you'll convert malformed-JSON throws into silent `null`s, which is a real behaviour change:

```ts
let text: string;
try {
  text = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8");
} catch {
  return null;
}
return text.length === 0 ? null : JSON.parse(text);
```

## Why not cache

Memoizing the parsed schema would flatten the p99 graph and leave the defect in place: the first request after every cold start, deploy, or cache eviction still eats all three blocking calls, and you've added a staleness bug on top — the file can change under a long-lived process with no invalidation path. Caching converts a per-request stall into a rarer, harder-to-reproduce stall. Fix the call, then cache later if profiling says the read is actually hot.

## Adjacent, deliberately out of scope

Two other findings sit in this same file and are worth knowing about before someone "fixes" the path while they're in here:

- `crime_00017` — `hardcoded_local_path`, evidence: "`/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9". `SCHEMA_PATH` points at one developer's machine, which is likely why the `existsSync` guard exists at all — the file is absent everywhere else, so `loadPlanSchema` returns `null` in every other environment.
- `crime_00016` — `hardcoded_localhost`, evidence: "`localhost:3000` @L8".

Neither is the latency bug. Both are separate changes.
